dot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" 13194139534901 17592186045417 true] [17592186443408 1072 #uuid "dd93c01d-3a4b-5e26-802f-bf34131bc696" 13194139534901 17592186045417 true] [17592186443408 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534901 17592186045417 true] [17592186443409 1058 #edb/ref 17592186443411 13194139534901 17592186045417 true] [17592186443409 1062 #uuid "46bb3e49-3990-5b69-ab08-fafc145eef1c" 13194139534901 17592186045417 true] [17592186443409 1063 #edb/ref 17592186443406 13194139534901 17592186045417 true] [17592186443410 1062 #uuid "5a94b1d8-d50b-579a-8ea5-b08b0054b5d6" 13194139534901 17592186045417 true] [17592186443410 1063 #edb/ref 17592186443407 13194139534901 17592186045417 true] [17592186443411 1058 #edb/ref 17592186443410 13194139534901 17592186045417 true] [17592186443411 1062 #uuid "d841447f-a108-5e27-b9e0-b6687617065c" 13194139534901 17592186045417 true] [17592186443411 1063 #edb/ref 17592186443408 13194139534901 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"activity-93b447e0-191c-560c-ac25-bc9695d12742" 17592186443315 "answer-014504c3-0095-55b6-8054-cf1b5f903c3b" 17592186443316 "answer-01fd3847-5484-52af-91c0-9b24a43a0040" 17592186443317 "answer-064fb74f-986e-5577-ad54-f76e5e51541d" 17592186443318 "answer-09d46260-ae76-5595-885c-d19b632acbfa" 17592186443319 "answer-0a9942cf-8883-5465-9f9d-7cad02983e8d" 17592186443320 "answe